          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9a11b59ea377be5c3820f4da\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But for me, he never would have gotten out. Morgan le Fay hated him with her whole heart, and she would never have softened toward him. Still, his crime had been done more out of carelessness than out of real wickedness. He had said she had red hair. Well, she did, but that was not a way to talk about it. If someone with red hair is above a certain social rank, the color is called auburn.\\n\\nThink about it. Among the forty-seven captives, there were five whose names, crimes, and dates of imprisonment were no longer known. One woman and four men\u2014old, bent, wrinkled, and so mentally worn down that they were almost gone. They had long ago forgotten the details themselves. At best, they had only vague ideas, and nothing they could say with certainty. Even the priests who had been responsible for praying with the captives every day and reminding them that God had put them there for some wise reason had only half-remembered stories. They taught patience, humility, and submission to oppression, especially for people of a lower rank, and they had traditions about these poor, ruined old bodies\u2014but nothing more. Those traditions only covered how long the imprisonment had lasted, not what the offenses were. Even with tradition, the only thing that could be proved was that none of the five had seen daylight for thirty-five years. How much longer that darkness had continued was impossible to guess. The king and queen knew nothing about these people, except that they were heirlooms\u2014assets passed down with the throne from the old firm. No history had been handed down with them, so the new owners had treated them as having no value and had felt no interest in them. I said to the queen:\\n\\n\u201cThen why in the world didn\u2019t you set them free?\u201d\\n\\nThe question was hard to answer. She didn\u2019t know why she hadn\u2019t. It had never even occurred to her. And now she was standing there, predicting the real history of future prisoners at the Castle d\u2019If, without realizing it. It was clear to me then that, with her upbringing, those inherited prisoners were only property\u2014nothing more, nothing less. And when you inherit property, you don\u2019t think to throw it away, even if you don\u2019t care about it.\\n\\nWhen I brought my procession of human bats out into the open world, into the glare of the afternoon sun\u2014after first covering their eyes, in kindness, because they had been denied light for so long\u2014they were something to see. Skeletons, scarecrows, goblins, pitiful terrors, each one of them. They were the most proper possible children of Monarchy by the Grace of God and the Established Church.\"}"
