{"request_id":"1189e244441f650c5f830e40","rewritten_text":"It struck me that this little lie was told in the simplest and prettiest fashion; yet I had only heard it once, and that surely changes how it lands—fresh, it must have been a pleasure to the others, no doubt.

Sir Dinadan the Humorist was the first to stir, and he soon got the rest going with a practical joke of such poor workmanship that it was almost impressive. He fastened some metal mugs to a dog’s tail and let the creature loose, and the animal tore around and around the place in a frenzy of terror, while all the other dogs bellowed after him and battered and crashed into whatever lay in their path, until the whole scene became a tangle of confusion and a din so deafening that it seemed to shake the air. At that, every man and woman in the crowd laughed until the tears ran, and some even tumbled from their chairs and rolled on the floor in sheer delight. It was just like a crowd of children. Sir Dinadan was so pleased with what he’d done that he couldn’t stop himself from repeating—again and again, to the point of weariness—how the immortal notion had first come to him; and, as humorists of his sort always do, he was still chuckling at it after everyone else had finished. So worked up was he that he decided to make a speech—naturally, a humorous one. I don’t think I ever heard so many old, worn-out jokes strung together in my life. He was worse than the minstrels, worse than the clown in the circus. It seemed especially pitiful to sit here, thirteen hundred years before I was born, and listen once more to those poor, flat, worm-eaten jokes that had given me the dry gripes when I was a boy thirteen hundred years afterward. It nearly convinced me that there’s no such thing as a new joke. Everyone laughed at these relics—though they always do; I’d noticed that, centuries later. Still, of course the scoffer didn’t laugh—I mean the boy. No, he scoffed; there wasn’t anything he wouldn’t scoff at. He said most of Sir Dinadan’s jokes were rotten, and the rest were petrified. I said “petrified” was a fine word; for I myself believed the only proper way to sort the grand ages of some of those jokes was by geologic periods. But that tidy notion struck the boy in a blank spot, since geology hadn’t been invented yet. Still, I made a note of his remark, and figured I’d try to educate the commonwealth up to it, if I managed to pull through. There’s no point throwing away a good thing just because the market isn’t ready.

Now Sir Kay rose and started firing at his history-mill with me as fuel."}

