st not be left longer unattended.\u201d\\n\\nFrom her reluctance to admit how much her closeness with Mr. Darcy had been exaggerated, Elizabeth had never yet replied to Mrs. Gardiner\u2019s long letter; but now, having that to communicate which she knew would be most welcome, she was almost ashamed to discover that her uncle and aunt had already lost three days of happiness, and wrote immediately as follows:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI would have thanked you sooner, my dear aunt, as I ought to have done, for your long, kind, and satisfactory account of particulars; but, to be honest, I was too vexed to write. You supposed more than really existed. But now, suppose as much as you please; give your fancy free rein, and let your imagination run in every direction the subject will allow. And unless you believe me actually married, you cannot be very far wrong.\u201d\"}"
