{"request_id":"a8f9fb52ec1ec03b6de8a6ee","rewritten_text":"It seemed to me that this quaint lie was told in the simplest and most beautiful way; but then I had only heard it once, and that makes a difference. It was probably pleasant to the others while it was still fresh.

Sir Dinadan the Humorist was the first to wake, and he soon got the rest up with a practical joke of sufficiently poor quality. He tied some metal mugs to a dog’s tail and let him loose, and the dog tore around and around the place in a frenzy of fright, while all the other dogs bellowed after him and battered and crashed into everything that got in their way, making a complete chaos of confusion and a most deafening din and turmoil. At that, every man and woman in the crowd laughed until the tears ran, and some even fell out of their chairs and rolled on the floor in pure ecstasy. It was just like so many children. Sir Dinadan was so proud of his stunt that he couldn’t stop himself from telling it over and over again, to the point of exhaustion, how the immortal idea had occurred to him; and, as humorists of his kind always do, he was still laughing at it after everyone else had finished. He was so worked up that he decided to make a speech—of course, a humorous one. I don’t think I ever heard so many old, worn-out jokes strung together in my life. He was worse than the minstrels, worse than the clown in the circus. It felt especially sad to sit here, thirteen hundred years before I was born, and listen again to those poor, flat, worm-eaten jokes that had given me the dry gripes when I was a boy thirteen hundred years afterward. It almost convinced me that there isn’t any such thing as a new joke possible. Everyone laughed at these antiques—but then they always do; I had noticed that, centuries later. Of course, the heckler didn’t laugh—I mean the boy. No, he scoffed; there wasn’t anything he wouldn’t scoff at. He said most of Sir Dinadan’s jokes were rotten, and the rest were petrified. I said “petrified” was a good word; as I believed, the only proper way to classify the majestic ages of some of those jokes was by geologic periods. But that neat idea hit the boy in a blank spot, because geology hadn’t been invented yet. Still, I made a note of the remark and calculated to educate the commonwealth up to it if I managed to pull through. There’s no point throwing away a good thing just because the market isn’t ready yet.

Now Sir Kay rose and began to fire up on his history-mill with me for fuel."}

