          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"daaa5a75a46055e44fbbf50c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But for me, he never would have managed to get out. Morgan le Fay had hated him with all the strength of her heart, and she would never have grown gentler toward him. Still, his wrongdoing had been carried out more through thoughtlessness than through any deliberate wickedness. He had said she had red hair. She did; but it was not the sort of thing to mention. When people with red hair rise above a certain social standing, their color turns auburn.\\n\\nThink of it: among those forty-seven captives there were five whose names, crimes, and dates of imprisonment were no longer known! One woman and four men\u2014each bent, and wrinkled, and with minds extinguished. They had long ago lost even the particulars; at best they held only hazy notions about them, nothing definite and nothing they would repeat twice in the same fashion. The line of priests who had been tasked to pray each day with the captives, to remind them that God had placed them there for some wise end, and to teach them that patience, humility, and submission to oppression were what He liked to see in those of a subordinate rank, had their own traditions about these poor old wrecks of humanity\u2014but nothing further. Those traditions went only so far as the length of the confinement, not the names of the offenses. And even with tradition to lean on, the only thing that could be proved was that none of the five had seen daylight for thirty-five years; how much longer that deprivation had continued was anyone\u2019s guess. The king and the queen knew nothing of these wretched creatures, except that they were heirlooms\u2014assets passed down, along with the throne, from the previous firm. No account of their past had been handed down with them, so the inheriting owners had judged them worthless and felt no curiosity about them. I asked the queen:\\n\\n\u201cThen why, in the world, didn\u2019t you set them free?\u201d\\n\\nThe question left her baffled. She couldn\u2019t say why she hadn\u2019t; it had never even occurred to her. And yet here she was, predicting the very real history of future prisoners of the Castle d\u2019If, without knowing it. It was plain to me now that, with her upbringing, those inherited prisoners were nothing but property\u2014no more, no less. For when we inherit property, we don\u2019t think to throw it away, even if we don\u2019t value it.\\n\\nWhen I brought my procession of human bats into the open world and the glare of the afternoon sun\u2014after first blindfolding them, in charity for eyes so long untroubled by light\u2014they were a sight to behold. Skeletons, scarecrows, goblins, pitiful terrors, each and every one; the most legitimate possible children of Monarchy by the Grace of God and the Established Church.\"}"
