          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b04d43550f6d8f6f64facc3f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But I keep wondering how long you would have gone on, if you had been left to yourself. I wonder when you would have spoken, if I had not asked you. My decision to thank you for your kindness to Lydia certainly had an effect. I\u2019m afraid it had too much; because what happens to the moral lesson, if our comfort comes from breaking a promise? I shouldn\u2019t have brought it up. This will never do.\\n\\n\u201cYou don\u2019t need to upset yourself. The moral will be fair enough. Lady Catherine\u2019s attempts to separate us only helped clear away all my doubts. I\u2019m not grateful for my happiness because you were eager to show your thanks. I wasn\u2019t in the mood to wait for you to give me an opening. My aunt\u2019s good sense gave me hope, and I decided right away to find out everything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLady Catherine has been very helpful, and that should make her happy, since she likes to be useful. But tell me\u2014why did you come down to Netherfield? Was it only to ride to Longbourn and feel awkward, or did you plan something more serious?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy real purpose was to see you, and to judge whether I could ever hope to make you love me. What I said to you, and what I told myself, was that I wanted to find out whether your sister still felt something for Bingley. If she did, I would make the confession to him that I have since made.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWill you ever have the courage to tell Lady Catherine what is going to happen?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m more likely to need time than courage, Elizabeth. But it must be done. If you will give me a sheet of paper, I\u2019ll do it at once.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd if I didn\u2019t have a letter to write myself, I could sit with you and watch how steady your writing is, the way another young lady once did. But I have an aunt too, and I can\u2019t neglect her any longer.\u201d\\n\\nBecause she had never wanted to admit how much her closeness with Mr. Darcy had been exaggerated, Elizabeth had not yet replied to Mrs. Gardiner\u2019s long letter. But now she had something to send that she knew would be especially welcome, and she was almost ashamed to realize that her uncle and aunt had already lost three days of happiness. She wrote at once as follows:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI would have thanked you sooner, my dear aunt, as I should have, for your long, kind, and satisfying account of everything. But to be honest, I was too upset to write. You assumed more than was really true. Still, suppose as much as you like. Let your imagination run free, and enjoy every possible idea the subject gives you. Unless you truly believe I am already married, you can\u2019t be very far wrong.\u201d\"}"
